L1: A geometric definition of Lie derivativefor Spinor Fields
L2: Lorenzo FATIBENE, Marco FERRARIS,Mauro FRANCAVIGLIA and Marco GODINA
L3: Istituto di Fisica Matematica “J.-L. Lagrange”Università di Torino, Via C. Alberto 10, 10123 TORINO (ITALY)
L4: Summary: Relying on the general theory of Lie derivatives a new geometric definition of Lie derivative for general spinor fields is given, more general than Kosmann’s one. It is shown that for particular infinitesimal lifts, i.e. for Kosmann vector fields, our definition coincides with the definition given by Kosmann more than 20 years ago.
L5: 1 Introduction
L6: It is known that starting from some works by physicists (Dirac [1,2], Pauli [3]) the notion of spinor was introduced in Physics, although its discovery is due to E. Cartan in 1913 (cf. [4]) in his researches on the linear representations of some simple Lie groups.
L7: A pretty nice and detailed algebraic theory was given later by C. Chevalley [5] and other papers on Clifford-algebras and spinor groups were published, in particular by J. Dieudonné [6], Atiyah, Bott, Shapiro [7], Milnor [8].
L8: Spinors on curved space-time manifolds were introduced and used since 1928 by physicists. In a series of very important papers Yvette Kosmann [9,10,11,12] introduced the notion of Lie derivative for spinor fields on spin manifolds and the study of the problem of transforming a spinor field under one-parameter group of diffeomorphisms of the base manifold.
L9: However, thanks to her beautiful and relevant work, we have the feeling that Kosmann’s theory was not formulated in the clearest possible way and that the geometric meaning of her “ad hoc” definition was still lacking. In fact, it is quite known that the difficulty in defining the Lie derivative of a spinor field on a space (and time) oriented (pseudo) Riemannian manifold (M, g) comes from the fact that there is no natural definition of the image of such a spinor field by a diffeo-
L10: This paper is in final form and no version of it will be submitted for publication elsewhere.
L11: morphism. This corresponds to the fact that the bundle SO(M, g) together with any spin bundle Spin(M, g) covering SO(M, g) are not natural bundles [17].
L12: Of course, these bundles are particular gauge-natural bundles [17,21] and we will show moreover that it is always possible to lift in a unique way any vetor field \xi \in \mathfrak{X}(M) of the base manifold (M, g).
L13: The corresponding vector field \tilde{\xi}_K \in \mathfrak{X}(Spin(M, g)), projectable over \xi, and \xi_K \in \mathfrak{X}(SO(M, g)) also projectable over \xi, will be called the Kosmann vector fields of \xi \in \mathfrak{X}(M). We remark that the vector field \tilde{\xi}_K also projects over \xi_K.
L14: The Kosmann lifting is not natural and differs from the Levi-Civita lifting, i.e. the principal lifting induced by the Levi-Civita connection on SO(M, g) (first order derivatives of \xi are involved), although the Ricci rotation coefficients of the Levi-Civita connection appear in the expression which gives the Kosmann lifting of \xi.
L15: In this paper we shall give a new definition of Lie derivative for spinor fields and we will show that for particular infinitesimal lifts, i.e. for Kosmann vector fields, our definition coincides with the definition given by Kosmann more than 20 years ago.
L16: 2 Spin structures
L17: Let (P, M, \pi, G) be a principal fiber bundle over M with structural group a Lie group G. Let \rho: \Gamma \rightarrow G be a central homomorphism of a Lie group \Gamma onto G with kernel K, that is K is discrete and contained in the center of \Gamma (see Greub and Petry in [13]; see also [22]). We recall that a \Gamma-structure on P is a principal bundle map \eta: \tilde{P} \rightarrow P which is equivariant under the right actions of the structure groups, that is:
L18: \eta(\tilde{z} \cdot \gamma) = \eta(\tilde{z}) \cdot \rho(\gamma) \quad , \tilde{z} \in \tilde{P}, \gamma \in \Gamma, \quad (2.1)
L19: and where \rho is assumed central by definition.
L20: Equivalently the following diagram commutes:
L21: \begin{array}{ccc} \tilde{P} \times \Gamma &amp; \xrightarrow{\eta \times \rho} &amp; P \times G \\ \downarrow \tilde{R} &amp; &amp; \downarrow R \\ \tilde{P} &amp; \xrightarrow{\eta} &amp; P \\ \downarrow \tilde{\pi} &amp; &amp; \downarrow \pi \\ M &amp; \xrightarrow{id_M} &amp; M \end{array} \quad (2.2)
L22: where R and \tilde{R} denote right multiplication in P and \tilde{P} respectively .
L23: This means that for \tilde{z} \in \tilde{P}, both \tilde{z} and \eta(\tilde{z}) lie over the same point, and that \eta restricted to a fiber is a “copy” of \rho, i.e. equivalent to \rho.
L24: In general, it is not guaranteed that a manifold admits a spin structure. The existence condition for a \Gamma-structure on P can be formulated [13,14,22] in terms of Čech cohomology.
L25: Let us also recall that for any principal fiber bundle (P, M, \pi, G) an (principal) automorphism of P is a diffeomorphism \phi: P \rightarrow P such that \phi(u \cdot g) = \phi(u) \cdot g,
L26: for every u \in P, g \in G. Each \phi induces a unique diffeomorphism \varphi: M \rightarrow M such that \pi \circ \phi = \varphi \circ \pi. Accordingly, we denote by Aut(P) the group of all principal (i.e. equivariant) automorphisms of P. Assume a vector field X \in \mathfrak{X}(P) on P generates the one-parameter group \phi_t. Then, X is G-invariant if and only if \phi_t is an automorphism of P for every t \in \mathbb{R}. Accordingly, we denote by \mathfrak{X}_G(P) the Lie algebra of G-invariant vector fields of P.
L27: Let \mathbb{R}^{p,q} be the familiar vector space \mathbb{R}^n (p+q=n) equipped with the canonical nondegenerate symmetric bilinear form \langle, \rangle of signature (p, q), i.e. with p “+” signs and with q “-” signs. We will denote by O(p, q) the (pseudo)-orthogonal group with respect to \langle, \rangle, that is O(p, q) = \{L \in Gl(\mathbb{R}^{p,q}) | \langle Lu, Lv \rangle = \langle u, v \rangle\}, by SO(p, q) = \{L \in O(p, q) | \det(L) = 1\} its special subgroup, and by SO_0(p, q) its connected component with identity. In the Euclidean case (p or q = 0), SO(p, q) = SO_0(p, q) but in the general case they are not equal. To define the Clifford algebra Cl(p, q) \equiv Cl(\mathbb{R}^{p,q}) we choose any orthonormal basis e_a of \mathbb{R}^{p,q} \subset Cl(p, q), that is \langle e_a, e_b \rangle = \eta_{ab}. The Clifford algebra Cl(p, q) is the real vector space endowed with an associative product generated (as an algebra) by the unit I and the elements e_a, 1 \leq a \leq n = p + q, with the relations:
L28: e_a e_b + e_b e_a = -2\eta_{ab} I \quad (2.3)
L29: where \eta_{ab} = 0 if a \neq b, +1 if a = b \leq p, and -1 if p &lt; a = b. As a real vector space, the Clifford algebra Cl(p, q) has dimension 2^n.
L30: The set \{I, e_a, e_{a_1} e_{a_2}, \dots, e_{a_1} e_{a_2} \dots e_{a_r}, e_{a_1} \dots e_{a_n} : a_1 &lt; a_2 &lt; \dots &lt; a_r\} forms a basis of Cl(p, q). The complexified Clifford algebra \mathcal{Cl}(p, q) is the vector space Cl(p, q) \otimes_{\mathbb{R}} \mathcal{C}, that is the vector space over the complex numbers generated by the previous elements I, e_a, \dots, e_{a_1} \dots e_{a_n}. In this paper we shall assume that the dimension of \mathbb{R}^n is even, that is n = 2d. In this case it can be proved that \mathcal{Cl}(p, q) is isomorphic to the complex algebra M_{2^d}(\mathcal{C}) of complex matrices of order 2^d. The matrices representing the fundamental elements e_a will be denoted by \gamma_a. They automatically carry a representation of various subgroups of Cl(p, q) and in particular of Spin(p, q).
L31: Let us briefly recall how Spin(p, q) is defined. Consider the multiplicative group of units of Cl(p, q), which is defined to be the subset
L32: Cl^\times(p, q) \equiv \{a \in Cl(p, q) : \exists a^{-1} \text{ such that } a^{-1}a = aa^{-1} = 1\} \quad (2.4)
L33: This group contains all elements v \in \mathbb{R}^{p,q} \subset Cl(p, q) with \langle v, v \rangle \neq 0 so it is possible to consider the subgroup generated by its “units length” elements, v \in \mathbb{R}^{p,q} \subset Cl(p, q) with \langle v, v \rangle = \pm 1.
L34: This subgroup it is denoted by Pin(p, q). The spin group Spin(p, q) is defined as the subgroup of Pin(p, q) consisting of even elements:
L35: Spin(p, q) = Pin(p, q) \cap Cl^0(p, q) \quad , \quad (2.5)
L36: where Cl^0(p, q) is a subalgebra of Cl(p, q) called the even part of Cl(p, q).
L37: Now, consider a space (and time) oriented (pseudo)-Riemannian manifold with metric g and dimension n = \dim(M). In this case we have G = SO(p, q), \Gamma = Spin(p, q), P = SO(M, g) \subset L(M), \tilde{P} = Spin(M, g) where Spin(p, q) is the usual notation for Spin(\mathbb{R}^{p, q}) and (L(M), GL(n), M) is the principal bundle of linear frames. We shall say that a spin-structure on a SO(p, q)-bundle is a Spin(p, q)-structure with respect to the covering of Lie groups \rho: Spin(p, q) \rightarrow SO(p, q) (see Milnor in [8]). For more details in Clifford-algebras and spinor groups see also [7,14,15,16]. When a spin structure is given \eta: Spin(M, g) \rightarrow SO(M, g), the principal bundle Spin(M, g) is sometimes called the bundle of spinor frames.
L38: Let us also recall that, in this case, the local isomorphism between Spin(M, g) and SO(M, g) allows the Levi-Civita connection to be lifted to a connection on the principal bundle Spin(M, g), equivariant by Spin(p, q), p + q = n.
L39: Let \omega: T[SO(M, g)] \rightarrow \mathfrak{so}(p, q) be the connection one-form determined by the Levi-Civita connection of (M, g). The corresponding connection \sigma on Spin(M, g)
L40: \sigma: T[Spin(M, g)] \rightarrow \mathfrak{spin}(p, q)
L41: is defined by the composition:
L42: \sigma = (\rho')^{-1} \circ (\eta^* \omega) \quad (2.6)
L43: where \rho' = T_e \rho: \mathfrak{spin}(p, q) \rightarrow \mathfrak{so}(p, q) is the Lie algebras isomorphism induced by \rho.
L44: Spinors are defined via a linear representation of the spin group Spin(p, q) on a complex vector space S, with \dim_{\mathbb{C}} S = 2^d.
L45: In fact, let \chi: Spin(p, q) \times S \rightarrow S be a spinor representation:
L46: \chi: Spin(p, q) \times S \rightarrow S : (h, s) \mapsto \chi(h, s) \equiv h \cdot s \quad (2.7)
L47: We recall that any representation of SO(p, q) yields a representation of its covering group, just by composition with \rho. We shall call a representation of this kind a tensor representation of Spin(p, q). A spinor representation of Spin(p, q) (sometimes called a double representation of SO(p, q)) is by definition a linear representation of Spin(p, q) which cannot be obtained from a representation of SO(p, q).
L48: Finally, consider the vector bundle S(M) \equiv Spin(M, g) \times_{\mathbb{C}^d} \mathbb{C}^d / Spin(p, q) over M associated to the principal fiber bundle Spin(M, g), where the action of Spin(p, q) on \mathbb{C}^d is the fundamental one defined by inclusion of Spin(p, q) in the complexified Clifford algebra \mathcal{Cl}(p, q). Then a spinor field \psi is a section x \mapsto [\tilde{e}_x, \Psi(\tilde{e}_x)] of S(M), where \Psi: Spin(M, g) \rightarrow \mathbb{C}^d, \Psi(\tilde{e}_x \cdot h) = h^{-1} \cdot \Psi(\tilde{e}_x), is an equivariant function, i.e. an assignment of components \Psi(\tilde{e}_x) in \mathbb{C}^d to each “spinor frame” \tilde{e}_x.
L49: 3 Lie derivative of Spinor Fields
L50: Given a spin structure Spin(M, g) \rightarrow M, a generalized spinorial transformation \Phi of Spin(M, g) is an automorphism \Phi: Spin(M, g) \rightarrow Spin(M, g). As discussed above \Phi is a diffeomorphism of Spin(M, g) such that \Phi(\tilde{e} \cdot h) = \Phi(\tilde{e}) \cdot h, for every \tilde{e} \in Spin(M, g), h \in Spin(p, q). Each \Phi induces a unique diffeomorphism \varphi: M \rightarrow M such that \eta_M \circ \Phi = \varphi \circ \eta_M, with \eta_M = \pi \circ \eta. Moreover, in this
L51: case, \Phi also induces a unique automorphism (i.e. equivariant diffeomorphism) \phi: SO(M, g) \rightarrow SO(M, g) such that \eta \circ \Phi = \phi \circ \eta. Equivalently we have the following diagram:
L52: \begin{array}{ccc} Spin(M, g) &amp; \xrightarrow{\Phi} &amp; Spin(M, g) \\ \downarrow \eta &amp; &amp; \downarrow \eta \\ SO(M, g) &amp; \xrightarrow{\phi} &amp; SO(M, g) \\ \downarrow \pi &amp; &amp; \downarrow \pi \\ M &amp; \xrightarrow{\varphi} &amp; M \end{array} \tag{3.1}
L53: Furthermore, let S(M) be the fiber bundle of spinors associated to the principal bundle Spin(M, g). For every element \Phi \in Aut(Spin(M, g)) we obtain an automorphism \Phi_{S(M)} of S(M), \Phi_{S(M)}: S(M) \rightarrow S(M), given by \Phi_{S(M)}(z) = [\Phi(\tilde{e}), s], z = [\tilde{e}, s] \in S(M), which is well defined because it does not depend on the representative chosen. In fact, this is so since \Phi is equivariant under the right action of the structure group Spin(p, q). A vector field \tilde{\Xi} \in \mathfrak{X}(Spin(M, g)) generating a one-parameter group of automorphisms of Spin(M, g) defines the generalized Lie derivative of any section \psi: M \rightarrow S(M), that is of any spinor field, as follows:
L54: \tilde{\mathcal{L}}_{\tilde{\Xi}}\psi = T\psi \circ \xi - \tilde{\Xi}_{S(M)} \circ \psi \quad , \tag{3.2}
L55: where \xi \in \mathfrak{X}(M) is the only vector field such that T\pi \circ \tilde{\Xi} = \xi \circ \pi, \tilde{\Xi} is the only vector field such that T\eta \circ \tilde{\Xi} = \tilde{\Xi} \circ \eta, and for \tilde{\Xi}_{S(M)} we have: \tilde{\Xi}_{S(M)}(z) = \frac{d}{dt}[(\Phi_t)_{S(M)}(z)]|_{t=0}, for every z \in S(M).
L56: Once again we have the following diagram:
L57: \begin{array}{ccc} Spin(M, g) &amp; \xrightarrow{\tilde{\Xi}} &amp; T[Spin(M, g)] \\ \downarrow \eta &amp; &amp; \downarrow T\eta \\ SO(M, g) &amp; \xrightarrow{\Xi} &amp; T[SO(M, g)] \\ \downarrow \pi &amp; &amp; \downarrow T\pi \\ M &amp; \xrightarrow{\xi} &amp; TM \end{array} \tag{3.3}
L58: Hence the generalized Lie derivative of \psi with respect to \tilde{\Xi}, \tilde{\mathcal{L}}_{\tilde{\Xi}}\psi: M \rightarrow V[S(M)], takes values in the vertical sub-bundle V[S(M)] \subset T[S(M)] and being S(M) a vector bundle there is a canonical identification V[S(M)] = S(M) \oplus S(M). In this case, (3.2) is of the form \tilde{\mathcal{L}}_{\tilde{\Xi}}\psi = (\psi, \mathcal{L}_{\tilde{\Xi}}\psi).
L59: The first component being the original section \psi, the second component \mathcal{L}_{\tilde{\Xi}}\psi is also a section of S(M), and it is called the Lie derivative of \psi with respect to \tilde{\Xi}. Sometimes, the second component \mathcal{L}_{\tilde{\Xi}}\psi is called the restricted Lie derivative [17]. Using the fact that the second component of \tilde{\mathcal{L}}_{\tilde{\Xi}}\psi is the derivative of (\Phi_{-t})_{S(M)} \circ \psi \circ \varphi_t for t = 0 in the classical sense, one can re-express the restricted Lie derivative in the form
L60: (\mathcal{L}_{\tilde{\Xi}}\psi)(x) = \lim_{t \rightarrow 0} \frac{1}{t} ((\Phi_{-t})_{S(M)} \circ \psi \circ \varphi_t(x) - \psi(x)) \quad . \tag{3.4}
L61: Further details and deeper discussions on the general theory of Lie derivatives may be found in [17,18,19].
L62: We are now seeking a coordinate expression for the restricted Lie derivative given by the equation (3.4). Now, if (x^\lambda, \psi^i) denote local fibered coordinates for S(M) then (3.4) reads as:
L63: \mathcal{L}_{\Xi} \psi = [\xi^a \mathbf{e}_a \psi^i - \tilde{\Xi}_j^i \psi^j] \mathbf{f}_i \quad , \quad (3.5)
L64: where \xi = \xi^a(x) \mathbf{e}_a, e = (\mathbf{e}_a) = (e_a^\mu(x) \partial_\mu) is a local section of SO(M, g) induced by a local section of Spin(M, g) \tilde{e}: U \rightarrow Spin(M, g) such that \eta \circ \tilde{e} = e, \mathbf{e}_a(\psi^i) is the Pfaff derivative of \psi^i, that is \mathbf{e}_a \psi^i = e_a^\mu(x) \partial_\mu \psi^i and \mathbf{f}_i is a basis of [S(M)]_x at each x \in U.
L65: Recalling that \tilde{\Xi} projects on \Xi and taking into account the Lie algebras isomorphism \rho' = T_e \rho: \mathfrak{spin}(p, q) \rightarrow \mathfrak{so}(p, q), we find the following relations:
L66: \tilde{\Xi}_j^i = -\frac{1}{4} \Xi^a{}_b (\gamma_a \gamma^b)_j^i = -\frac{1}{4} \Xi_{ab} (\gamma^a \gamma^b)_j^i \quad (3.6a)
L67: \tilde{\Xi}^a = \Xi^a = \xi^a \quad (3.6b)
L68: where indices in (3.6a) are lowered and raised with respect to the (pseudo) Riemannian metric g, i.e. \eta_{ab} = g(\mathbf{e}_a, \mathbf{e}_b). Recall that for \Xi, being a SO(p, q)-invariant vector field of SO(M, g), in the orthonormal basis (e_a^\mu(x) \partial_\mu), we have \Xi_{ab} = -\Xi_{ba}. We also note that the sign in (3.6a) depends on the choice of sign for the Clifford algebra, and the choice between L^a{}_b \gamma_a and L_b{}^a \gamma_a, where L^a{}_b and L_b{}^a are any matrices.
L69: Finally, for the spin connection coefficients we have:
L70: \sigma_{jc}^i = -\frac{1}{4} \omega^a{}_{bc} (\gamma_a \gamma^b)_j^i = -\frac{1}{4} \omega_{abc} (\gamma^a \gamma^b)_j^i \quad . \quad (3.7)
L71: So we can re-write the (3.5) as follows:
L72: \mathcal{L}_{\Xi} \psi = \{ \xi^a \nabla_a \psi^i - [-\frac{1}{4} (\omega_{[abc]} \xi^c + \Xi_{ab}) (\gamma^a \gamma^b)_j^i \psi^j] \} \mathbf{f}_i \quad , \quad (3.8)
L73: where in the orthonormal frame (e_a^\mu(x) \partial_\mu) we have \omega_{abc} = -\omega_{bac} and square brackets on indices mean complete antisymmetrization, although in this case \omega_{[abc]} = \omega_{abc}. In fact, the coefficients \omega_{abc} are the Ricci rotation coefficients defined by:
L74: \omega_{abc} = \eta_{ad} \omega^d{}_{bc} \quad . \quad (3.9)
L75: Moreover, for \nabla_a \psi^i we have the local expression:
L76: \nabla_a \psi^i = \mathbf{e}_a \psi^i + \sigma_{ja}^i \psi^j \quad . \quad (3.10)
L77: In this notation we have, in the same orthonormal basis (e_a^\mu(x) \partial_\mu), the following: \nabla_{\mathbf{e}_a} \mathbf{e}_b = \omega^c{}_{ba} \mathbf{e}_c for the connection coefficients and \omega^a{}_b = \omega^a{}_{bc}(x) \theta^c for the connection one-forms, where \theta^c is the dual basis of \mathbf{e}_a. Hence, in our notation we get for the covariant derivative \nabla_a \xi_b the following local expression:
L78: \nabla_a \xi_b = \mathbf{e}_a \xi_b - \omega^c_{ba} \xi_c = \mathbf{e}_a \xi_b - \omega_{cba} \xi^c \quad , \quad (3.11)
L79: where \xi_c = \eta_{cb} \xi^b.
L80: Given a space (and time) oriented (pseudo) Riemannian manifold (M, g) and a vector field \xi it is possible to give its lift to SO(M, g), denoted by \xi_K. The vector field \xi_K has the following coordinated expression:
L81: \xi_K = (\xi_K)^a \mathbf{e}_a + (\xi_K)_{ab} \mathbf{A}^{ab} \quad , \quad (3.12)
L82: where the coefficients (\xi_K)^a and (\xi_K)_{ab} are given by:
L83: (\xi_K)^a = \xi^a \quad (\xi_K)_{ab} = -\{\nabla_{[a} \xi_{b]} + \omega_{[ab]c} \xi^c\} \quad , \quad (3.13)
L84: and where \nabla_{[a} \xi_{b]} = \mathbf{e}_{[a} \xi_{b]} - \omega_{c[ba]} \xi^c. The vector fields \mathbf{A}^{ab} are local right SO(p, q)-invariant vector fields on SO(M, g) and in a suitable chart (x^\mu, u_a^b) are defined as follows:
L85: \mathbf{A}^{ab} = \frac{1}{2}(\eta^{ac} \delta_d^b - \eta^{bc} \delta_d^a) u_e^d \frac{\partial}{\partial u_c^e} \quad . \quad (3.14)
L86: In order to define local coordinates (x^\mu, u_a^b) which appear in (3.14) we remind that, as above, the local section e: U \rightarrow SO(M, g) is induced by a local section of Spin(M, g) \tilde{e}: U \rightarrow Spin(M, g), so that we have \eta \circ \tilde{e} = e. Moreover, if (x^\mu) are local coordinates of a chart of the base manifold M with the same domain U, we can define local coordinates u_a^\mu by (u_a^\mu(u) \partial_\mu) where u = (\mathbf{u}_a) is any frame in the open \pi^{-1}(U) of L(M) such that g(\mathbf{u}_a, \mathbf{u}_b) = \eta_{ab}. Finally, with the local section e: U \rightarrow SO(M, g) we define local coordinates u_a^b by \mathbf{u}_a = u_a^b \mathbf{e}_b.
L87: Accordingly, \xi_K transforms as a SO(p, q)-invariant vector field on SO(M, g) and projects over \xi. An alternative geometric definition of \xi_K is the following one. Given any vector field \xi of the base manifold M, it admits a unique natural lift \hat{\xi} to the linear frame bundle L(M). Then, \xi_K is by definition the skew of \hat{\xi} with respect to the (pseudo) Riemannian metric g.
L88: Furthermore, when a spin structure is given \eta: Spin(M, g) \rightarrow SO(M, g), any vector field \xi lifts to a vector field on the spin bundle Spin(M, g). In fact, its lift \tilde{\xi}_K is defined by:
L89: \tilde{\xi}_K = (\tilde{\xi}_K)^a \mathbf{e}_a + (\tilde{\xi}_K)_j^i \mathbf{E}_i^j \quad , \quad (3.15)
L90: and the coefficients (\tilde{\xi}_K)^a, (\tilde{\xi}_K)_j^i are given by:
L91: (\tilde{\xi}_K)^a = \xi^a \quad (\tilde{\xi}_K)_j^i = -\frac{1}{4}(\xi_K)_{ab} (\gamma^a \gamma^b)_j^i \quad , \quad (3.16)
L92: so that \tilde{\xi}_K projects over \xi_K and hence over \xi. Accordingly, the following diagram commutes:
L93: \begin{array}{ccc} Spin(M, g) &amp; \xrightarrow{\tilde{\xi}_K} &amp; T[Spin(M, g)] \\ \downarrow \eta &amp; &amp; \downarrow T\eta \\ SO(M, g) &amp; \xrightarrow{\xi_K} &amp; T[SO(M, g)] \\ \downarrow \pi &amp; &amp; \downarrow T\pi \\ M &amp; \xrightarrow{\xi} &amp; TM \end{array} \tag{3.17}
L94: The vector field \tilde{\xi}_K is well defined because the one-parameter group of automorphisms \{\phi_t\} of \xi_K can be lifted (see below) to an one-parameter group of automorphisms \{\tilde{\phi}_t\} so that \tilde{\xi}_K(\tilde{e}) = \frac{d}{dt}[\tilde{\phi}_t(\tilde{e})]|_{t=0}, for every \tilde{e} \in Spin(M, g).
L95: The vector fields \tilde{\xi}_K, \xi_K, invariant under the actions of Spin(p, q) and SO(p, q), are called the Kosmann vector fields of the vector field \xi. Let us also remark that the Kosmann lifting \xi \mapsto \xi_K is not a Lie-algebra homomorphism although the following remarkable relation holds:
L96: [\xi, \zeta]_K = [\xi_K, \zeta_K] - \frac{1}{2} \mathcal{L}_\xi(g_{\lambda\mu}) g^{\mu\nu} \mathcal{L}_\zeta(g_{\nu\sigma}) \mathbf{A}^{\lambda\sigma} \tag{3.18a}
L97: where
L98: \mathbf{A}^{\lambda\sigma} = e_a^\lambda e_b^\sigma \mathbf{A}^{ab} . \tag{3.18b}
L99: We are now in a good position to state the following result.
L100: Proposition. The Lie derivative \mathcal{L}_\xi\psi of a spinor field \psi: M \rightarrow S(M) with respect to a vector field \xi \in \mathfrak{X}(M), as defined by Kosmann in [9], is the second component of the generalized Lie derivative of \psi with respect to the Kosmann lift \tilde{\xi}_K of \xi. That is if \tilde{\mathcal{L}}_{\tilde{\xi}_K}\psi is the generalized Lie derivative of \psi and \mathcal{L}_{\tilde{\xi}_K}\psi its second component then we get \mathcal{L}_\xi\psi = \mathcal{L}_{\tilde{\xi}_K}\psi.
L101: Proof: It is an immediate consequence of the above considerations and from the fact that the local expression of the Lie derivative given by Kosmann is
L102: \mathcal{L}_\xi\psi = [\xi^a \nabla_a \psi^i - \frac{1}{4} \nabla_{[a} \xi_{b]} (\gamma^a \gamma^b)^i_j \psi^j] \mathbf{f}_i . \tag{3.19}
L103: (Q.E.D)
L104: We remark that the above proposition gives us a geometric meaning to the Kosmann's formula (3.19) which is taken by Kosmann as a definition of the Lie derivative of \psi with respect to \xi. We shall use the following notation: \tilde{\mathcal{L}}_\xi\psi := \tilde{\mathcal{L}}_{\tilde{\xi}_K}\psi. We finally remark that there is an easier interpretation of the above facts in the case in which \xi is an infinitesimal isometry, i.e. a Killing vector field on M.
L105: In fact, given a spin structure \eta: Spin(M, g) \rightarrow SO(M, g), let us consider a Killing vector field \xi, its flow denoted by \{\varphi_t\} and the principal bundle map \phi_t: SO(M, g) \rightarrow SO(M, g) which is defined by restriction of L_{\varphi_t}: L(M) \rightarrow L(M) to SO(M, g). The automorphisms \phi_t are well defined since the diffeomorphisms \varphi_t are isometries. Moreover being \eta: Spin(M, g) \rightarrow SO(M, g) a covering space it
L106: is possible to lift \phi_t to a bundle map \tilde{\phi}_t: Spin(M, g) \rightarrow Spin(M, g) in the following way. For any spinor frame \tilde{e} \in Spin(M, g) over \eta(\tilde{e}) = e, from the theory of covering spaces it follows that for the curve \gamma_e: \mathbb{R} \rightarrow SO(M, g), based at e, that is \gamma_e(0) = e, and defined by \gamma_e(t) := \phi_t(e) there exists a unique curve \tilde{\gamma}_{\tilde{e}}: \mathbb{R} \rightarrow Spin(M, g), based at \tilde{e}, such that \eta \circ \tilde{\gamma}_{\tilde{e}} = \gamma_e. It is possible to define a principal bundle map \tilde{\phi}_t: Spin(M, g) \rightarrow Spin(M, g), covering \phi_t, by letting \tilde{\phi}_t(\tilde{e}) := \tilde{\gamma}_{\tilde{e}}(t). The one-parameter group of automorphisms of Spin(M, g) \{\tilde{\phi}_t\} defines a vector field \tilde{\Xi}(\tilde{e}) = \frac{d}{dt}[\tilde{\phi}_t(\tilde{e})]|_{t=0}, for all \tilde{e} \in Spin(M, g).
L107: It is then easy to verify that the vector field \tilde{\Xi} coincides with the Kosmann vector field \tilde{\xi}_K of the vector field \xi. In fact, in this case, the lift \tilde{\xi} is already skewsymmetric. Hence the Lie derivative of \psi with respect to \xi is still given by the Kosmann's formula (3.19) (cf. [23,24]) or, better, by the following coordinate-free expression
L108: (\mathcal{L}_\xi \psi)(x) = \lim_{t \rightarrow 0} \frac{1}{t} ((\tilde{\phi}_{-t})_{S(M)} \circ \psi \circ \varphi_t(x) - \psi(x)) \quad . \quad (3.20)
L109: Moreover, even for an arbitrary vector field \xi the formula (3.20) is still valid but where now \{\phi_t\} is the flow of the vector field \xi_K, and \{\tilde{\phi}_t\} is the flow of \tilde{\xi}_K. Now, \xi is not an infinitesimal isometry but \xi_K generates a one-parameter group of principal automorphisms of SO(M, g) which lift to Spin(M, g) so that even in this case (3.20) gives us a further interpretation of Kosmann's formula, i.e. a direct classical definition of the Lie derivative for spinor fields.
